  "schemaVersion": "1.3",
  "kind": "archsim.architecture",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "Checkout call chain: deadlines and retries per hop",
    "description": "A checkout behind an Envoy ingress, across four services that each own their data: a Node.js storefront BFF, a Java orders service (200 Tomcat threads per pod), a Python pricing service that stores each quote in PostgreSQL, and a Go inventory service that reserves stock in DynamoDB. Orders keeps order history in PostgreSQL behind a Redis cache. A web shop (45%) and a mobile app (55%) send 600 req/s; a quarter are checkouts, which call pricing through an Envoy sidecar's route, then inventory, then commit the order. Order-history reads never touch pricing. As drawn, three rules hold: deadlines shrink at every hop (the apps 8 s and 5 s, the ingress 3 s, BFF \u2192 orders 2.5 s, pricing 800 ms, 500 ms for each store), only the outermost caller retries (the mobile app, once, with an idempotency key on checkouts), and every call has a timeout. The engine calls a service's dependencies one after another, as this code does; parallel fan-out is not modelled. The scenarios make pricing answer 5 s late for 30 s and break one rule at a time. All numbers are illustrative assumptions.",
    "tags": [
      "microservices",
      "timeouts",
      "retries",
      "circuit-breaker",
      "envoy",
      "deadlines",
      "client-groups"
    ]
  },
